{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["The meaning of <b>and one who strikes an animal</b>: a second time, to mention the stranger, because the infliction of a wound applies to an Israelite, for thus it is written 'of his fellow' — only one who strikes a man or an animal, <b>one law for them, for the stranger and for the citizen.</b> And I have already informed you that when two minims combine it is a concise form, and Scripture too adopted a concise form, in that it did not state with 'one who strikes a man and he dies,' for it is known that the striker is not put to death until the death of the one struck. And another interpretation: it mentioned 'a soul' at the beginning, the meaning being that he struck him in a life-threatening place, and afterward stated a general rule: whoever kills shall be put to death, whether he struck in a life-threatening place or not in a life-threatening place; therefore it did not mention 'soul.'"], :book :leviticus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:12:14.039919652Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}